module extdeps.pin




data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority = extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority {
  uri: extdeps.uri.Uri {
    scheme: extdeps.uri.Https
    locator: "reproducible-builds.org/docs/definition/"
  }
}

data pin_citation_note: String = "Cites the Reproducible Builds definition, whose stated relevant attributes of a build environment include 'dependencies and their versions' — which is exactly what this module models: a dependency named by an exact version identity plus the expected content identity of the artifact, so a build's inputs are recreatable rather than whatever the host happened to have. The authority is deliberately LANGUAGE-AGNOSTIC and SUBJECT-AGNOSTIC. Cargo.lock and flake.lock are named in pin_selection_note as PRECEDENTS for the discipline, which is the honest role for them: instances of the practice, not the authority over it. Precedent for a concept-level anchor in extdeps: extdeps.version cites en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_versioning rather than any one ecosystem's version scheme. Not en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lock_file — that page is file locking for mutual exclusion, a homonym, not dependency pinning. WHY THIS MODULE AND ITS CliTool INSTANTIATION EACH SPELL THEIR OWN ANCHOR RATHER THAN ONE REFERENCING THE OTHER, carried forward from the pre-split carrier because it is MEASURED and would otherwise be rediscovered the hard way: review 44329 proposed referencing a sibling module anchor instead of re-spelling it, and that remedy is NOT AVAILABLE — src/v2/lens/mandatory_tag reads the anchor structurally from the Node tree, so an aliased declaration reds corpus_live_clean_tree_wall_holds. Two anchors here are therefore two distinct CITATIONS (this module cites the reproducible-builds discipline; extdeps.tools.pin cites the GNU command-line conventions that make a CliTool a distinct subject), not one fact spelled twice, and the structural read is why neither can be an alias of the other."

data pin_cited_symbol_verification_note: String = "review 44920, and the discipline it forced, recorded because the defect recurred three times in one PR and prose is where it hides. THE DEFECT: this carrier's notes instruct a future P2 worker to call a specific function, and TWO of the names asserted were wrong — observed_tool_identity_for, which never existed (I invented the _for suffix without reading the declaration), and observe_tool, which #7444 renamed to observe_tool_identity. Both were fabricated in the strict sense DESIGN section 5 means: a described surface never checked against the surface. It is worse than a typo because the sentence is an INSTRUCTION — a worker following it would grep, find nothing, and either guess or re-derive a digest, which is precisely the section 3 fork these notes exist to prevent. THE DISCIPLINE, applied to every symbol in this module rather than only the two reported: each cited name was grep-verified against dag/ and src/ to have a real declaration. ObservedToolIdentity, ObservedToolIdentityFound, ObservedToolIdentityMissing, ObservedToolIdentityDuplicate, observed_tool_identities, observed_tool_identity, toolchain_identity and ResolvedBuildContext all resolve to dag/extdeps/realization/emit_on_demand_host.dag; membership_reconcile to dag/gunbc/membership_reconcile.dag:59; corpus_live_clean_tree_wall_holds to dag/tools/extdeps_external_authority_transport.dag; observe_tool_identity to src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs:8672. THE DISCIPLINE WAS NOT ENOUGH, AND THAT IS THE POINT (review 45088): every name above resolves, but the LINE beside the last one did not — 8598 is inside emit_host_artifact_realization_digest, an unrelated artifact-store helper, and observe_tool_identity actually begins at 8672 with its digest folded at 8712-8719. The stale pointer was copied into gunbc.design_document as this row's evidence, so a name-level grep passed while the located claim was false in the canonical design authority. A line number is the one part of a citation that grep-verifying the SYMBOL cannot check and that ordinary edits above it silently invalidate, which is exactly why the remedy below is a resolver rather than more diligence. WHY THIS IS A LENS CANDIDATE AND NOT A HABIT: a note naming a symbol is an ordinary reference that the substrate could resolve, so a stale citation is DECIDABLE — it is the same Node-tree read the namespace authority already performs, applied to identifiers appearing inside note strings. Today nothing checks it, which is why three of them shipped. dissolve-on: feature:cited-symbol-resolution — a lens resolves identifiers cited in note strings against the containment tree and reds an unresolvable one; at that point the NAME half of this note deletes and that class becomes unwritable rather than reviewed. THE TRIGGER AS ORIGINALLY WRITTEN WOULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT review 45088, and saying otherwise would overclaim it: an identifier resolver checks names against the .dag containment tree, while the defect was a LINE pointer into a .rs file — outside that tree entirely, so no amount of identifier resolution reds it. The two halves are separate classes with separate remedies. dissolve-on: feature:cited-source-location-checked — a cross-language file:line citation is verified against the named file, either by resolving the symbol the line is claimed to inhabit or by deleting the line half so the SYMBOL is the whole citation. The second shape is the cheaper and likelier fix and it is worth naming as the default: a function name is stable under edits above it, a line number is not, so a citation that carries only the name is the one that stays true. This note deletes when both triggers have fired."

data pin_dimension_note: String = "PINNING IS A DIMENSION, NOT A PROPERTY OF CLI TOOLS (operator, 2026-07-29). The first version of this model was a ToolPin record keyed by tool_name: NonEmptyStr, which was wrong three ways and the operator caught it by reading the shape. (1) It referred to a tool by NAME STRING while extdeps.tools already passes the modeled row by value everywhere else — resolve(tool: CliTool), ResolvedTool { tool: CliTool }, NotFound { tool: CliTool } — so ToolPin was the single site in that namespace joining to a modeled type through string equality, the anemic-leaf failure of DESIGN section 2 with tool identity then living in two places. (2) It fixed the GRAIN at CLI tools, when the corpus already contains other pinnable subjects that are not CliTool at all: extdeps.cache.sccache SccacheBinaryArtifact (release x published musl arch — NOT SccacheBinaryRelease, which carries two digests per row and is ambiguous at Pin grain), extdeps/github ActionRef, extdeps/docker and extdeps/container/docker_ce images, apt packages, GitHub releases. A type per pinnable thing is the same mistake as ten hand-rolled integer types before Compose<Int, MachineWidth<N>> — N types where there is one axis. (3) Naming the type after its first consumer is how the grain got fixed too narrow, and the general shape was available from the start. So Pin is parameterized by its SUBJECT, following the Vendor<Domain> precedent (dag/extdeps/vendor.dag): the subject is a type argument, the pin is one concept at every breadth. Pin<CliTool>, Pin<SccacheBinaryArtifact>, Pin<ActionRef>, Pin<ContainerImage> are instantiations, not siblings."

data pin_subject_must_not_be_self_identifying_note: String = "review 44850, and the reviewer found a real defect in the previous version of this proof. THE SOUNDNESS CONDITION ON THE DIMENSION, sharpened after review 44999 and deep-eagle-728 oracle msg_fac61885. Two subject cases, two carriers, one axis — duplication unwritable rather than forbidden-after-the-fact. (1) Subject does NOT carry content identity (CliTool, ActionRef): Pin<Subject> with required version and expected_identity — the pin SUPPLIES identity the subject lacks; expected_identity is new information with one home. (2) Subject fully determines cited digest identity for its grain (SccacheBinaryArtifact — release x published musl arch, not the ambiguous SccacheBinaryRelease row): SubjectProjectedPin<Subject> carries subject + selection only; identity and version are projected by the instantiation module and supplied at the reconcile bridge — never stored twice on the pin carrier. Pin<OciDescriptor> copying descriptor.digest into expected_identity was case (2) wrongly modeled as case (1) — two independently writable fields for one fact. OCI descriptors with ContentHash on the subject remain dissolve-on feature:pin-subject-self-identity-lens targets; they must not be Pin<Subject> instantiations. DECIDABILITY: 'subject declares ContentHash' is structural inspection; until the lens lands, review plus the SubjectProjectedPin split for digest-on-subject artifacts is the disposition. What is NOT claimed: that Pin<OciDescriptor> literals are unwritable today — saying otherwise would be the section 5 trap of editing a declaration while the realization lies."

data pin_authority_note: String = "The single authority for pinned dependency identity. Pin<Subject> names an exact VersionIdentity selection and expected ContentHash — BOTH REQUIRED, never a location, never a range — so a digest-less or version-less Pin is unwritable rather than validated (DESIGN section 5 construction, not a post-check). Subjects that fully determine cited digest identity use SubjectProjectedPin instead; the instantiation bridge projects version and expected_identity explicitly onto Pin at the generic boundary (deep-eagle-728 oracle msg_fac61885) — Pin.version Absent is not representable. This module mints NO new digest concept: expected_identity is the same ContentHash that extdeps.realization.emit_on_demand_host produces on the OBSERVED side, where the per-tool authority is ResolvedBuildContext.observed_tool_identities and its rows are digested from logical name + executable bytes + version-probe output (#7388, per-tool rows named by #7444). Pin declares expected and that side observes actual, so this is one identity concept with two ends rather than a second authority. review 44920 caught an earlier version of this sentence naming toolchain_identity as the observed half. That is now WRONG in a way that would actively mislead: since #7444, toolchain_identity is a DERIVED FOLD over the per-tool rows, so a P2 worker reading only this note could wire a reconcile against the aggregate — the exact grain mismatch review 44388 raised and #7444 discharged. The observed half a pin compares against is a per-tool row, never the fold. It is NOT yet a reconcile, and this note deliberately does not say what the remaining gap IS: the grain relationship between the two ends, its history and its current status are owned entirely by pin_observed_grain_prerequisite_note below. review 44910 caught this row asserting the prerequisite still had to land while that note already recorded it discharged — two copies of one status in one module, which is the section 3 duplication this lane exists to remove, so the copy is deleted rather than re-synced. Re-syncing is what failed: the same defect was corrected twice on this PR before (review 44422, review 44875) by editing a second copy, and a fact with two homes will always drift again."

data pin_observed_grain_prerequisite_note: String = "review 44388 raised this and #7444 DISCHARGED it; the row is kept as the record of what the grain argument was, because the reconcile still has to honour it. THE ORIGINAL FINDING: expected_identity is PER SUBJECT, while ResolvedBuildContext.toolchain_identity was an aggregate — seeded from a constant and hash_combined over every argv in build_argvs, plus a second observe_tool_identity call for rustc whenever the argv was cargo — so it moved when an unrelated tool in the same build moved and could not say WHICH dependency drifted. Comparing one Pin against it would have been a grain mismatch wearing the shape of a reconcile. WHAT LANDED (#7444, merged 2026-07-29, dag/extdeps/realization/emit_on_demand_host.dag): ResolvedBuildContext now carries observed_tool_identities: List<ObservedToolIdentity> as its first field, toolchain_identity is re-expressed as the derived FOLD over those rows rather than a stored aggregate, and the per-tool read is observed_tool_identity with three arms — ObservedToolIdentityFound, ObservedToolIdentityMissing { tool_name }, ObservedToolIdentityDuplicate { tool_name, count }. The miss and the duplicate are DISTINCT typed answers, not one Option, which is what makes the lookup usable at a fail-closed boundary. So the two ends now meet at the same grain and no second per-subject digest was minted beside observe_tool_identity — the section 3 fork this lane exists to remove was avoided. WHAT IS STILL NOT WRITTEN: the reconcile itself. This carrier declares expected and #7444 observes actual; the gunbc.membership_reconcile instantiation that relates them, with a bundle whose value_eq is pin_value_eq and whose key_of is the per-subject projection, is P2 and belongs to no row here. A consumer relating the two MUST route through observed_tool_identity and answer its Missing and Duplicate arms explicitly — collapsing either into a match failure or a widened rerun would be the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 forbids, and would waste exactly the precision #7444 bought."

data pin_selection_note: String = "The refresh axis, which keeps a pin from being a freeze. Execution ALWAYS resolves an exact identity — there is no moving reference at run time, because resolving 'latest' at execution is nondeterminism at the substrate boundary (v2.std.determinism) and would make two runs of one spec disagree. How the exact identity was CHOSEN is the separable fact: ExactPin is authored directly; TrackedChannel records that the identity was resolved FROM a moving reference (a release channel, 'latest', a semver range), with a declared refresh window. It does NOT record the resolution DATE: no such field exists on the carrier today, and an earlier version of this sentence claimed one did — an absent fact described as modeled, which is the section 5 specification-without-execution trap (review 44422). The missing date is exactly what makes the refresh window undecidable today: TrackedChannel carries no resolution timestamp, so no freshness verdict is derivable and none is reported. Re-resolution is an explicit action producing a reviewable diff — the Cargo.lock / flake.lock shape. That is the INTENT of this axis; nothing here reports a pin current."

data pin_refresh_window_positivity_note: String = "review 44397, carried forward and now CLOSED AT LITERAL SITES by where-refinement enforcement (PR #7438). The finding was right that an unrestricted Int admits zero and negative refresh windows as writable substrate states. The field is therefore std.integer.PositiveInt (Nat where gt_zero), which puts the bound on the SINGLE EXISTING AUTHORITY for it — PositiveInt is already consumed by std.approximate_field, so no new bound, refinement, or validator is minted here (consume-never-fork). MEASURED after enforcement: constructing TrackedChannel { channel: \"latest\", refresh_window_days: 0 } against this carrier is now a typed TypeMismatch at compile time — the where-refinement on PositiveInt is enforced by the v1 typechecker at int-literal construction sites only, and dag/std/integer.dag remains its only declaration site. The wall arrives from the single authority rather than a local check, which is precisely why the bound belongs on PositiveInt. RESIDUE (wall after grounding, not a ratchet): non-literal int values at refined positions still compile with a counted WhereRefinementUnenforced advisory per site — the check is decidable (evidence present or not) but the evidence carrier (refinement subtyping / parameter evidence) is not yet modeled; hard-refusing every non-literal site was measured and broke dag_compile_clean across Octet/Port/Milliseconds migration surfaces (b8a4df4 predecessor). Advisory count is observable via the compile_clean_diagnostic_histogram bin (WhereRefinementUnenforced rows keyed by predicate and reason); a new deferral reason cannot ship silently — it must be enrolled in is_interpreter_blocking_diagnostic and is_discovery_corpus_advisory_typecheck_diagnostic in src/v1/00_core.dag or compile-clean policy shifts without review. The lifecycle condition for that residue is NOT stated here: it is a typed row in pin_frontier_rows, so this note explains and does not legislate."

type PinSelection
  = ExactPin
  | TrackedChannel {
      channel: std.types.NonEmptyStr
      refresh_window_days: std.integer.PositiveInt
    }

type Pin<Subject> {
  subject: extdeps.coverage.Subject
  version: extdeps.version.VersionIdentity
  expected_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash
  selection: extdeps.pin.PinSelection
}

data subject_projected_pin_note: String = "When a pin subject fully determines cited digest identity for its grain, Pin<Subject> would duplicate one fact in independently writable fields — the OciDescriptor failure class and review 44999 on SccacheBinaryArtifact. SubjectProjectedPin carries subject + selection only; identity and version are projected by the instantiation module and supplied at the reconcile bridge — never stored twice (construction, deep-eagle-728 oracle msg_36c1fcf1). admit_subject_projected_pin_integrity and subject_projected_pin_value_eq live HERE as the single admission and value_eq authority for every SubjectProjectedPin instantiation; instantiation modules own projection fns (content_identity, pin_version, key_of) only. P2 membership_reconcile bridges via subject_projected_pin_as_pin(pin, expected_identity, version) — both fields passed explicitly, symmetric with identity projection, so Pin.version Absent is never representable (msg_fac61885 regression fix). Pin<Subject> over subjects that declare ContentHash remain dissolve-on feature:pin-subject-self-identity-lens targets."

// A MOVING REFERENCE IS NOT A PIN SUBJECT, and this is the carrier for the case Pin<Subject> and
// SubjectProjectedPin both refuse. Pin supplies an identity the subject lacks and requires an exact
// VersionIdentity beside it; SubjectProjectedPin applies when the subject itself determines the
// identity. An upstream TAG is neither: `gpt-oss:20b` carries no version (upstream models it as a
// tag with no version field, so a VersionIdentity would be locally coined), and it does not determine
// its content -- one ref has now projected to two manifest identities over time, which is the whole
// incident this carrier exists to make expressible.
//
// So the two facts are separated: the moving REFERENCE is the selection source, and the exact
// IDENTITY is the selected result. The relation between them is a resolution that happened at a
// moment, on evidence -- not a property of either end.
//
// FIRST_EVIDENCE IS NOT OPTIONAL, which is the construction claim. A resolution with no evidence is
// an assertion about upstream bytes with nothing behind it, and that is exactly the shape that let an
// authored literal travel as an "observed" digest. Making the evidence a required field means an
// evidence-free resolution has no constructor rather than failing a check.
type TrackedReferenceResolution<Reference, Evidence> sole_constructor {
  reference: v2.lens.grounding_ledger.Reference
  resolved_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash
  resolved_at: std.types.Timestamp
  first_evidence: Evidence
  further_evidence: std.types.List<Evidence>
}

// The only mint. sole_constructor already refuses construction outside this module; this is the
// surface every instantiation routes through, so the required-evidence argument holds at one place.
fn tracked_reference_resolution<Reference, Evidence>(
  reference: v2.lens.grounding_ledger.Reference,
  resolved_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  resolved_at: std.types.Timestamp,
  first_evidence: Evidence,
  further_evidence: std.types.List<Evidence>,
) -> TrackedReferenceResolution<v2.lens.grounding_ledger.Reference, Evidence> {
  TrackedReferenceResolution {
    reference: reference,
    resolved_identity: resolved_identity,
    resolved_at: resolved_at,
    first_evidence: first_evidence,
    further_evidence: further_evidence,
  }
}

// Every evidence reading in one list, primary first, so a consumer counting confirmations does not
// have to know that the first one is stored in a different field than the rest.
fn tracked_reference_resolution_evidence<Reference, Evidence>(
  resolution: TrackedReferenceResolution<v2.lens.grounding_ledger.Reference, Evidence>,
) -> std.types.List<Evidence> {
  concat([resolution.first_evidence], resolution.further_evidence)
}

type SubjectProjectedPin<Subject> {
  subject: extdeps.coverage.Subject
  selection: extdeps.pin.PinSelection
}

type SubjectProjectedPinAdmission<Subject>
  = SubjectProjectedPinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: extdeps.pin.SubjectProjectedPin<extdeps.coverage.Subject> }
  | SubjectProjectedPinRefused { subject: extdeps.coverage.Subject, cause: extdeps.pin.PinRefusalCause }

fn subject_projected_pin_as_pin<Subject>(
  pin: extdeps.pin.SubjectProjectedPin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>,
  expected_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  version: extdeps.version.VersionIdentity,
) -> extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.coverage.Subject> {
  Pin {
    subject: pin.subject,
    version: version,
    expected_identity: expected_identity,
    selection: pin.selection,
  }
}

fn admit_subject_projected_pin_integrity<Subject>(
  pin: extdeps.pin.SubjectProjectedPin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>,
  observed_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
  expected_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
) -> extdeps.pin.SubjectProjectedPinAdmission<extdeps.coverage.Subject> {
  match pin.selection {
    TrackedChannel { channel: c, refresh_window_days: _ } =>
      SubjectProjectedPinRefused {
        subject: pin.subject,
        cause: TrackedResolutionUnrecorded { channel: c },
      }
    ExactPin =>
      match compare_content_hash(left: expected_identity, right: observed_identity) {
        ContentHashEqual =>
          SubjectProjectedPinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: pin }
        ContentHashDifferent =>
          SubjectProjectedPinRefused {
            subject: pin.subject,
            cause: ObservedIdentityMismatch {
              expected: expected_identity,
              observed: observed_identity,
            },
          }
        ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable =>
          SubjectProjectedPinRefused {
            subject: pin.subject,
            cause: CrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable {
              expected: expected_identity,
              observed: observed_identity,
            },
          }
      }
  }
}

fn subject_projected_pin_value_eq<Subject>(
  a: extdeps.pin.SubjectProjectedPin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>,
  b: extdeps.pin.SubjectProjectedPin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>,
) -> Bool {
  (a.subject == b.subject) && (a.selection == b.selection)
}

data integrity_versus_currency_note: String = "Two questions were once fused under the word 'fresh', and separating them is load-bearing (review 44274). INTEGRITY asks: is the artifact in front of us the one this pin declares? That is decidable NOW — compare the observed digest to expected_identity — and it is what admission establishes. CURRENCY asks: is the declared version still the one we should be running? That is NOT decidable here for EITHER variant, and an earlier shape fabricated a positive answer for one of them: ExactPin returned PinFresh unconditionally. Authorship proves reproducibility, not currency — an exact pin authored three years ago is exactly as reproducible and exactly as rotten, and reporting it fresh forever is the 'pinned and never updated' failure this lane's refresh axis exists to prevent (operator's framing when the axis was requested: 'some way to avoid pinning and never updating'). So no arm anywhere returns a positive currency verdict: pin_currency_gap is TOTAL and always names a gap, and the word 'fresh' does not appear in this module. A consumer that needs currency reads that gap and refuses; it cannot be handed a green it did not earn."

type CurrencyGap
  = AuthoredPinHasNoReviewDate
  | TrackedPinHasNoResolutionDate { channel: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

fn pin_currency_gap<Subject>(pin: extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>) -> extdeps.pin.CurrencyGap {
  match pin.selection {
    ExactPin => AuthoredPinHasNoReviewDate
    TrackedChannel { channel: c, refresh_window_days: _ } =>
      TrackedPinHasNoResolutionDate { channel: c }
  }
}

data pin_refusal_cause_note: String = "Every cause is SUBJECT-AGNOSTIC, which is why they live on the generic carrier rather than beside any one instantiation. ConstraintIsNotIdentity and PinAbsent are the two ingest deficits — a range is not an identity, and no version stated at all is a DIFFERENT deficit from a stated range, so collapsing them would be a state-space conflation. TrackedResolutionUnrecorded and ObservedIdentityMismatch are the two admission refusals on Pin<Subject>. CitedDigestIdentityUnprojectable is the SubjectProjectedPin admission refusal when the subject's cited digest cannot be bridged to ContentHash (review 45470 — sha256_digest_content_hash Absent must refuse, never absorb a hardcoded fallback). CrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable is the sixth: expected and observed are both real identities but sit in different hash families, so no comparison is defined and answering `false` would be a fabricated mismatch rather than a refusal. All six name their deficit and are countable, so a migration frontier burns down visibly instead of being silently assumed complete. Subjects that fully determine cited digest identity use SubjectProjectedPin instead (no second writable expected_identity field); review 44999 and deep-eagle-728 oracle msg_36c1fcf1 — a PinRestatesSubjectMismatch validation arm had no live producer for shipped CliTool/ActionRef subjects and would have been coverage-by-illusion."

type PinRefusalCause
  = ConstraintIsNotIdentity { constraint: extdeps.version.VersionConstraint }
  | PinAbsent
  | TrackedResolutionUnrecorded { channel: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | CitedDigestIdentityUnprojectable
  | CrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable { expected: std.content_hash.ContentHash, observed: std.content_hash.ContentHash }
  | ObservedIdentityMismatch { expected: std.content_hash.ContentHash, observed: std.content_hash.ContentHash }

// PinRefused carries the SUBJECT, not a name string. The first shape carried tool_name: NonEmptyStr, which re-spelled the subject's identity in the refusal and forced every consumer to join back by string equality — the same anemic-leaf defect the dimension note records for ToolPin itself. Carrying the subject means a refusal points AT the row that failed, so a consumer can project whatever identity it actually needs (a CliTool's name, a release's tag) from the modeled value rather than trusting a copied string.
type PinAdmission<Subject>
  = PinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.coverage.Subject> }
  | PinRefused { subject: extdeps.coverage.Subject, cause: extdeps.pin.PinRefusalCause }

data pin_integrity_admission_note: String = "Establishes INTEGRITY ONLY, and is named so it cannot be misread as currency (review 44274). PinIntegrityAdmitted asserts exactly one thing: the observed artifact is the one this pin declares. It asserts NOTHING about whether the declared version is still the right one — that is pin_currency_gap, which is total and always names a gap, so currency can never be silently inherited from an integrity pass. A TrackedChannel pin refuses integrity admission outright (review 44242): its expected_identity was resolved from a moving reference and no record of that resolution is carried, so there is no evidence the stored digest corresponds to any decision anyone made. ExactPin proceeds to the digest comparison, which is the real, decidable check. Both arms refuse rather than widen; neither substitutes a superset, silently re-resolves, nor proceeds on mismatch."

fn admit_pin_integrity<Subject>(
  pin: extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>,
  observed_identity: std.content_hash.ContentHash,
) -> extdeps.pin.PinAdmission<extdeps.coverage.Subject> {
  match pin.selection {
    TrackedChannel { channel: c, refresh_window_days: _ } =>
      PinRefused {
        subject: pin.subject,
        cause: TrackedResolutionUnrecorded { channel: c }
      }
    ExactPin =>
      match compare_content_hash(left: pin.expected_identity, right: observed_identity) {
        ContentHashEqual =>
          PinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: pin }
        ContentHashDifferent =>
          PinRefused {
            subject: pin.subject,
            cause: ObservedIdentityMismatch {
              expected: pin.expected_identity,
              observed: observed_identity,
            },
          }
        ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable =>
          PinRefused {
            subject: pin.subject,
            cause: CrossFamilyIdentityIncomparable {
              expected: pin.expected_identity,
              observed: observed_identity,
            },
          }
      }
  }
}

data pin_value_eq_note: String = "The bundle member the reconcile hands to gunbc.membership_reconcile as value_eq, and the reason it does NOT compare the subject. Identity-vs-content split per that spine's authority note: the SUBJECT is the stable identity and therefore the key_of, so a version, digest, or selection change is a Modified hunk and re-upserts — an ensure that reinstalls in place — rather than a Removed plus Added pair that would tear the dependency down and reinstall it. value_eq is therefore presence-plus-content over every field that changes what gets installed or when it is re-resolved: the pinned version, the expected identity, AND the selection policy. Comparing the subject here would be redundant with the key and would make every hunk a no-op. Selection is content, not metadata: flipping ExactPin to TrackedChannel, or widening a refresh window, changes the ensure behaviour with no version or digest movement, so omitting it would emit no hunk for a real drift (review 44329). expected_identity comparison routes through std.content_hash.compare_content_hash (review 45407) — the canonical typed outcome, not a consumer-local coproduct predicate. Selection is compared with the substrate's structural equality on the coproduct rather than a hand-written per-variant predicate — the closed vocabulary already decides it, so a predicate would be a second representation of a decision the substrate makes (review 44341). key_of is NOT declared here: projecting an identity out of a Subject is subject-specific, so it belongs with each instantiation, not on the generic carrier."

fn pin_value_eq<Subject>(a: extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>, b: extdeps.pin.Pin<extdeps.coverage.Subject>) -> Bool {
  (a.version == b.version)
    && match compare_content_hash(left: a.expected_identity, right: b.expected_identity) {
      ContentHashEqual => true
      ContentHashDifferent => false
      ContentHashCrossFamilyIncomparable => false
    }
    && (a.selection == b.selection)
}

data pin_frontier_rows: std.types.List<std.roster_frontier.FrontierRow> = [
  std.roster_frontier.frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.pin", decl_name: "PinSelection"),
    reason: "refresh_window_days is a bare PositiveInt count of days rather than a day-typed magnitude",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "feature:pin-refresh-window-measure — a calendar-day carrier exists to consume (std.measure stops at Minute: Nanosecond, Microsecond, Millisecond, Second, Minute — there is no Day, and a calendar day is not an SI scale of seconds, so this cannot be satisfied by picking an existing row); then refresh_window_days becomes that carrier and this row deletes. Consume-never-fork"),
  ),
  std.roster_frontier.frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.pin", decl_name: "pin_refresh_window_positivity_note"),
    reason: "the positivity wall holds at literal sites only; non-literal values at refined positions still compile with a counted advisory",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "feature:refinement-evidence-at-non-literal-positions — refinement subtyping / parameter evidence is modeled so a non-literal at a refined position either carries proof or hard-refuses; WhereRefinementUnenforced deferrals for gt_zero/range/non_empty at non-literal sites delete; the pin_refresh_window_positivity_note residue paragraph deletes with this row"),
  ),
  std.roster_frontier.frontier_row_decl(
    ref: decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.pin", decl_name: "pin_currency_gap"),
    reason: "TrackedChannel stores no resolution date, so no freshness verdict is derivable and none is reported",
    dissolution: unbound_dissolution(description: "feature:pin-tracked-freshness-evidence — v2.std.datetime gains day arithmetic (it has no day-difference, epoch-day, or days-between fn today, and zero CalendarDate constructors are in use anywhere in the corpus) AND TrackedChannel carries resolved_on: CalendarDate; then pin_currency_gap gains a genuine positive arm derived from stored evidence plus an observed date"),
  ),
]
